---
// The Charvaka thread (design §9.1). Unlisted: no nav entry. The page only
// opens for readers who have found all five fragments hidden in the comic.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { fragments } from "../lib/companions";
import { codexEntries } from "../lib/codex";

const charvaka = (await codexEntries()).find((e) => e.id === "charvaka");
---
<Base title="The suppressed thread" description="A thread running under the whole book, for readers who went looking.">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div id="locked" hidden>
      <p class="eyebrow">Not yet</p>
      <h1>Something is missing</h1>
      <p class="prose">You have found <strong id="count">0</strong> of {fragments.length} fragments. They are hidden in the pages of the comic,
        marked like this: <span class="mark">✦</span> — turn on <em>Notes</em> in the reader and look where the Professor is at his most careless.</p>
      <p><a class="btn" href="/read/">Back to the books</a></p>
    </div>

    <article id="unlocked" hidden>
      <p class="eyebrow">A thread you had to look for</p>
      <h1>Charvaka: the suppressed thread</h1>
      <p class="lede prose">Every other tradition in the valley gets a temple, a shrine or an ashram. This one gets a rumour, a burned reputation
        and a scholar who will not let it go. You found all {fragments.length} pieces of it.</p>

      <ol class="found">
        {fragments.map((f) => (
          <li>
            <h2>{f.title}</h2>
            <p>{f.text}</p>
            <p class="source muted">{f.source} · <a href={`/read/${f.page.slice(0, 2)}/${Number(f.page.split("-p")[1])}`}>see the page →</a></p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>

      <section class="prose">
        <h2>What it adds up to</h2>
        <p>The Charvakas, or Lokayatas, denied the afterlife, the authority of the Vedas and the point of ritual. Almost nothing of theirs survives:
          what we know comes mostly from opponents quoting them in order to refute them. In the valley that pattern repeats in miniature.
          The one philosophy with no monument has left only a half-hidden shrine, a greeting, and a set of manuscripts that a scholar has been
          quietly collecting.</p>
        <p>It also sets up the book's own question. The Mahant's authority rests on ritual and prophecy, exactly what Charvaka denounced as
          "a work of fools." And the Professor, who has found the texts, wonders aloud whether that puts him "under grave peril."</p>
        {charvaka && <p><a class="btn" href="/codex/charvaka/">The Codex entry on Charvaka</a></p>}
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>

<script>
  import { foundFragments, FRAGMENT_COUNT } from "../lib/fragments";
  const found = foundFragments();
  const done = found.length >= FRAGMENT_COUNT;
  document.getElementById(done ? "unlocked" : "locked")!.hidden = false;
  const count = document.getElementById("count");
  if (count) count.textContent = String(found.length);
</script>

<style>
  .lede { font-size: 1.2rem; }
  .mark { font-size: 1.2rem; color: var(--sepia); }
  .found { list-style: none; padding: 0; display: grid; gap: 18px; margin: 28px 0; }
  .found li { background: var(--paper-2); border-left: 4px solid var(--sepia); border-radius: 6px; padding: 14px 18px; }
  .found h2 { margin: 0 0 6px; font-size: 1.25rem; }
  .source { font-family: var(--font-ui); font-size: 0.85rem; }
</style>
